<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu'l-Khayr Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1412">1412</a>–<a href="/years/1468">1468</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Abulkhair Khan of the Uzbeks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu'l-Khayr Khan was a Shibanid descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> who ruled the nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> of the steppe east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-river">Ural River</a> from 1428 until his death. He united many tribes, took towns on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> and raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, but after a defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a> in 1457 his authority weakened. The sultans Kerei and Janibek broke away to found the Kazakh Khanate, and his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a> later conquered Transoxiana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abul-khayr-khan.jpg" alt="Abu'l-Khayr Khan" width="185" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abdulxair.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the mid-fifteenth century most of the steppe east of the Ural River was ruled by <b>Abu'l-Khayr Khan</b> of the Uzbeks, a Shibanid.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1500 Muhammad Shaybani, grandson of <b>Abu'l-Khayr</b> of the Uzbek Khanate, took Samarkand from the Timurids and soon conquered Transoxiana and Khurasan.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-bukhara">Khanate of Bukhara</a></li><li><blockquote>Shibanid princes ruled there, and the khan <b>Abu'l-Khayr</b> was proclaimed in this region in 1428 before moving south.</blockquote><a href="/state/tyumen-khanate">Tyumen Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Golden Horde collapsed, the Shibanid <b>Abu'l-Khayr Khan</b> proclaimed himself khan in 1428, founding the Uzbek Khanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/ulus-of-shiban">Ulus of Shiban</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1428 the Shibanid prince <b>Abu'l-Khayr</b> was proclaimed khan in western Siberia by the tribes of the former Ulus of Shiban and White Horde.</blockquote><a href="/state/uzbek-khanate">Uzbek Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>After further civil wars the eastern lands passed to Shibanid and other Jochid princes, notably <b>Abu'l-Khayr</b> of the Uzbek Khanate in 1428.</blockquote><a href="/state/white-horde">White Horde</a></li></ul></section>
